{"result":{"data":{"overall_correctness":"correct","explanation":"Inferred story: async encode changed the ownership boundary, not just the call syntax. Sync Tonic borrowed `&mut EncodeBuf`, completed the protobuf body before returning, then Tonic performed size/compression/header framing after the borrow ended. In async, encode may return `Pending`, so a borrowed `async fn encode(&mut self, ..., &mut EncodeBuf)` would leave the future holding stack borrows of `self`/`dst`; Tonic instead stores one `Send + 'static` owned `T::Encode` future per in-flight message, with the `EncodeBuffer` moved into that future and returned on `Ready` for Tonic to frame. Prost’s role is the finer checkpoint: inside the stored future it records field/index/phase/payload state so, for example, a length-delimited field that already emitted tag+length resumes only the payload copy. DSA/IAX makes that ownership physical because a pending hardware copy may still hold raw source/destination addresses and completion state after the Rust call returns; the owned future keeps source bytes, destination storage, and cleanup alive until ready/drop.

Understanding did not break. The main places that relied on prior knowledge were slide 3 (`Send + 'static` storage implications), slide 6 (why drop order is safety-critical), and slide 9 (what DSA/IAX concretely is). For the stated Rust async/Tonic/Prost audience, the deck is self-contained and the plan matches the inferred story.

No required fixes. Optional exact clarifications: on slide 3 add “Tonic’s body driver stores this future after the encode call returns”; on slide 6 change the red note to “Cleanup must run before item/buffer storage can be freed”; on slide 9 change the orange tag to “DSA/IAX = hardware copy engine holding raw src/dst pointers while Pending.”","confidence":0.89,"findings":[]}}}
